# Data Layer Conventions

Schema definitions live in `watchly-technical-plan.md` §4 — this file is about how to
work with them, not a second copy of the DDL.

- Core Prisma models: `User`, `Room`, `RoomParticipant`, `ChatMessage` (optional
  persistence — fine to skip writing these at MVP and rely on the realtime broadcast
  only), `Upload`. Don't add new top-level models for things that are really just
  fields on `Room` (e.g. video source config is a JSON field on `Room`, not its own table).
- `User.authUserId` references Supabase's `auth.users(id)` — every user (including
  anonymous/guest sign-ins) gets a real `auth.users` row from Supabase Auth, and a
  corresponding row in our own `users` table for app-specific fields (display name,
  avatar config). Don't treat guests as sessionless; they still need a stable id for
  seat assignment, chat attribution, and reconnect.
- `Room.videoSource` is a JSON blob: `{ type: 'link' | 'upload', url, uploadId }` — keep
  it a tagged union in the Prisma/TS types, don't split into nullable `linkUrl` /
  `uploadId` columns.
- IDs are UUIDs generated at the DB level (`gen_random_uuid()` / `@default(uuid())`),
  not app-generated — keeps insertion logic simple and avoids collision handling.
- `RoomParticipant` is an append-only join log (`joined_at`/`left_at`), not a
  single-row "current membership" table — current membership lives on the room's
  PartyKit party instance (see `realtime.mdc`), Postgres just needs the history.
- There is no Redis in this stack. Ephemeral, fast-changing room state
  (playback position, presence, seat map) lives on the PartyKit party, not here —
  if a value changes more than once every few seconds, it doesn't belong in Postgres.
- If using Supabase's client directly (instead of/alongside Prisma) for simpler reads,
  Row Level Security policies still need to enforce the same rules the server would —
  don't rely on the client only requesting what it's "supposed to."
- Migrations: every schema change goes through a Prisma migration file (or Supabase
  migration, if using the Supabase CLI), never a manual change against the dev or
  prod database.
